# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores LF for every text file and checks LF out on every platform, including
# Windows. This is deliberate rather than the git default: .editorconfig sets `end_of_line = lf`,
# and CI's Format Check runs `dotnet format --verify-no-changes`, which reads the working tree. On a
# Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true — the git-for-windows default — the working tree would be
# CRLF while .editorconfig demands LF, and the check reports tens of thousands of ENDOFLINE errors
# on a clean checkout. CI does not see this because it runs on Linux, so without this file the
# check is only runnable on half the machines that develop here.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binary content, so assets/icon.png and anything like it are
# left untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, stated explicitly so detection is never the thing standing between an image and a
# corrupting newline translation.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pfx binary
*.snk binary
*.zip binary
